    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the IRS, the great goblin overlord of tax rules! In the realm of accounting, when it comes to depreciating an asset like your ergonomic chair, you must indeed follow the IRS's guidelines. The IRS allows you to depreciate the cost of the chair over its useful life, which is typically 7 years for office furniture under the Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System (MACRS). This means you can spread the cost of the chair over several years, deducting a portion each year, rather than writing it off all at once. This way, you can claim a smaller deduction each year, but over a longer period, which can be beneficial for your tax situation. Just remember, in the land of taxes, following the rules is like following the map to a goblin's treasure – it might not be the quickest path, but it will lead you to the treasure you seek. So, consider consulting with a tax professional to ensure you're following the IRS's guidelines to the letter.",
